## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about Kubernetes resources and Spark on Kubernetes, and perform practical memory tuning with metrics, GC, and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes resource requests/limits
- Spark memory tuning (driver/executor memory, memoryOverhead, spark.memory.fraction)
- JVM GC options and GC logging
- Shuffle-heavy workloads and GC pressure
- Observability (Spark UI, kubectl top, metrics)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kubernetes patch example (pseudo)
const patch = {
  spec: {
    template: {
      spec: {
        containers: [
          { name: "executor",
            resources: { requests: { memory: "12Gi" }, limits: { memory: "12Gi" } }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect a memory leak vs normal GC pauses?
- How would you verify stability after changes with a canary run?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about secret management granularity, cache invalidation, and zero-downtime refresh.

## Key Concepts

- Secrets mounted as files via projected volumes
- In-app credential caching and refresh patterns
- Signals and inotify/fsnotify for reload
- Rolling updates vs hot reload

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js snippet: watch credential file and reload
const fs=require('fs');
let creds;
function load(){
  creds = fs.readFileSync('/var/secrets/db-creds','utf8');
}
load();
fs.watch('/var/secrets/db-creds',(ev, f)=>{ if(ev==='change'){ load(); /* refresh DB pool here */ }});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement a Kubernetes-native approach to trigger a reload when secret rotates without application changes?
- What are trade-offs between in-pod reload vs restart and how to monitor?
